{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 8, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize small-scale drug possession is ending Sunday, replaced by a new statute that reinstates penalties for holding substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Under HB4002, individuals caught with these drugs can face misdemeanor charges carrying up to six months in jail—or opt into a health-focused pathway involving structured support and behavioral interventions. The law also tightens restrictions on selling drugs near areas like playgrounds and schools, signaling a stronger enforcement stance.\n\n***\n\nPolice are now tasked with determining whether someone qualifies for an alternative route to incarceration. To qualify, a person must have no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, no other active charges, and be medically stable. If these conditions are met, officers contact a regional coordination line to summon a mobile health team. That team must arrive within half an hour; if they don’t, the individual will be taken into custody. There’s no room for delay—officers cannot wait indefinitely.\n\n***\n\nSupporters argue this shift allows local areas to tailor responses according to their capacity, avoiding a one-size-fits-all model. Critics, however, warn that without uniform standards, access to help will vary widely across regions, potentially leaving some vulnerable individuals behind. The success of the program hinges on timely, consistent support—and whether counties can scale services fast enough to meet demand.\n\n***\n\nIn a country where many struggle to access basic care, even simple acts like eating a meal or shopping for essentials can feel out of reach for those in crisis. A person’s age doesn’t shield them from the consequences of addiction, and sometimes, the hardest part isn’t the drug—it’s the silence 